<p>&#8220;Do philosophers really think differently than non-philosophers?&#8221;<span id="more-58342"></span></p>
<p>That&#8217;s the question taken up by a team of researchers in a recent study published in <em>Philosophical Psychology</em>. Focusing on differences between philosophers and non-philosophers in regard to neurodivergent traits and responses to thought experiments, the researchers found some support, in the aggregate, for a &#8220;yes&#8221; answer to that question.</p>
<p>The following guest post is by <a href="https://charleston.edu/philosophy/faculty/nadelhoffer-thomas.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas Nadelhoffer</a> (College of Charleston), and is based on the article, &#8220;<a href="https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/09515089.2026.2655817" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thinking Differently: Neurodivergent Traits and Responses to Thought Experiments in Philosophers and the General Population</a>,&#8221; co-authored with <a href="https://www.paulcmckee.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paul McKee</a> (Duke), <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/khaoulaboulhadi/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Khaoula Boulhadi</a> (Southern Connecticut State), and Helena Weymouth (College of Charleston).</p>
